<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabula Contrebiensis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">87 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tabula Contrebiensis is a bronze tablet of 87 BCE found in 1979 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botorrita">Botorrita</a>, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, it records a dispute between two neighbouring communities over land and a water channel, decided by the senate of Contrebia under the authority of the Roman governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-valerius-flaccus">Gaius Valerius Flaccus</a>. It is one of the earliest Roman legal documents from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
